 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127475 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127471 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186127476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127471 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127471 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127471 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0fa97c7f-7cc1-5ce8-a4a6-ef6136fcea7b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127472 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127472 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127472 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a9969e64-c9d8-55ff-b6ee-437dd5681197" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127473 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127473 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127473 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "adbc1161-dfad-5a69-a6e3-0913ea1df7c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127474 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127474 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127474 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b1a511c4-c1f9-516b-9dca-a07e7044681e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127475 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127475 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127475 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b895eb31-f0ab-5ec3-bb82-c9db0c01857e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127476 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{3}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127476 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186127476 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a87c994-be02-5a07-8109-69a2e56a1dac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128945 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128945 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5f/5f3cd6aeb514ee0a59d1f3534342f85e1c00743d9e7c7004b8aaf8ccfbe353f9.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1-4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128945 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1b13d9bb-9bcf-50be-a58d-39e82d99b9bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128945 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/9f/9fb15e52a1df912c9e5fb4238f45d705cd0a56fb7190620c0f0fd597f9a2b8c0.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/80/80bd36915851e6ebeb67befe8af41e5496fa5491b9d87c294d8b937eb586319f.png)\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186128946 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5531" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128946 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/0f/0f341a3893509e9adc2b5897e14a87656aa3c49824dda809ae6030646c6ece79.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PCP^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26e641c9-bda5-5b93-8137-f4ec744ddb5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128946 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/48/489a26b52ac132d82ffa918d931963a0cd33df092aea8e70885dc41c4e363be5.svg)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\n- From the matrix ![](images/bf/bfc2be5324b72df4c3c63e92c4501bbda6b0fcde0ef8e6284d739db51e73a722.svg) we deduce that $\\textrm{Re}(\\lambda)={\\color{blue}3}$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\lambda) = {\\color{red}-1}.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = {\\color{blue}3}+({\\color{red}-1})\\text{i} = 3-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix ![](images/1f/1fd5d191e88cd5f00ffd91f83800ce0c82024cedea60bf78432cd0abb504252a.svg) we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n![](images/df/df9978ae9ef1bb16b79e36abace84ef559ef64b6aa9e47369ae7efc5ecf7da27.svg)\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n![](images/81/811faf0baa4ae081f2f5ae9064a060f979ae553df5e5e156028e91b3038a4a2b.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128947 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128947 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/74/74cc68e763183d4599dc223cce44849ace88129bbc82b6d49bcbafc4a1a8163d.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A$ has the complex eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and that $A=PCP^{-1}$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, find the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128947 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75f39ec3-0963-5555-b418-d5b2df3be0ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128947 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/b5/b5c1766ea1d9eb7a913fd7be2d7bf71909c81da03aaff8f4c631e1a3955f9589.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nSince we are given the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-2+6\\textrm{i},$ we can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n![](images/7f/7f29c1c9be88268b14ef9131599420874fff6a399a3e3c92d3f4b635906163f6.png)\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions to the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda_1 =-2+6\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2+6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2+6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3-6\\text{i} & -5 \\\\ 9 & -3-6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(3-6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-5{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+(-3-6\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_1=\\dfrac 1 3(1+2\\textrm{i})x_2.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n![](images/ab/ab958a638d0bce3cb2498d6f64016e581bb303fd0df725abcfaa58dbd836b95d.png)\n\nSetting $x_2=3$, we get the eigenvector \n\n![](images/63/631f718ed2642536c5a8e02dedc38544af5e8b6cc48bff7a0afcf77c0d057d1b.png)\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129033 #edb/ref 1069 "e-8734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129033 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f4/f4a051da70308d92a5530fca3ba9c214cff919a6072e0e4d863f5aad10f405ee.png)\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2+4\\textrm{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PCP^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0469305-b1d9-5430-9911-2f0c209b3264" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129033 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/4a/4a53548a17d9b4fd870ce70285bfc2c57cf6c8d352f75149e5aa7511ef4af853.png)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n![](images/aa/aa5738b600440bf8327670fc21cd8005c660def232119b2b4808237d988367bd.png)\n\nwhile\n\n![](images/fc/fc91fcabd47720075061e670f62be9ea9b636070a05cba70752bb7060608b764.png)\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overline{\\lambda}=2-4\\textrm{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda.$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overline{\\mathbf{v}},$ the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}={{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129666 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186326740 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129134 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186362361 #edb/r