y $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & -7\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-7$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-7+(-2)\\text{i}=-7-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1+(-2)\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-2\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-7∓2\\text{i},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1∓2\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186326743 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326743 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326743 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326743 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186326743 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00ab985d-3ab3-5cde-bfdb-d8fae88f985d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326743 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}7-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +52 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=4\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4-6\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=4-6\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ 15 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4-6\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4-6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3+6\\text{i} & -3 \\\\ 15 & -3+6\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}(3+6\\text{i}){x}_{1}-3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ 15{x}_{1}+(-3+6\\text{i}){x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=(1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ (1+2\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1+2\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360831 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "00a864a5-90fe-5a19-9ac4-88e0769c548c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360839 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0232ac2d-f477-56d2-bc66-4e4e7ff088f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360872 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186360872 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186360872 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "099c80b1-ec77-53c5-9fec-f0d48316573a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361002 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{5}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361002 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361002 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "26967be4-a2cf-5a4e-8868-aa0a99d24c89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361221 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -1 \\\\ 8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186361221 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361221 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "554e46a6-951d-57f0-9e37-f12e320d01df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186361260 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{5}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1